{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And he said, Swear to me.</b> Joseph was not suspect in his eyes as one who would not fulfill the commandment of his father; rather, he had him swear in order that he might have an excuse before Pharaoh, as Rashi explained on the verse ״כַּאֲשֶׁר הִשְׁבִיעֶךָ״ (Genesis 50:5) — that were it not for the oath, I would not have let you go, etc. And as for what is said in the language of 'to me' and 'to him' — 'Swear to me' and 'he swore to him' — it appears to me that Jacob was afraid lest Pharaoh say to Joseph to release him from his oath; therefore he said, 'Swear to me,' that is, on my understanding, and one who swears on the understanding of his fellow cannot be released without the knowledge of his fellow, as is found in Tur Yoreh De'ah, sign 227. Therefore it says 'to me' — on my understanding, 'and he swore to him.'" "Another matter: since Joseph said, 'I will do as you have spoken,' and it is said (in Midrash Rabbenu Bachya, Vayehi, 47:30) that Joseph said, 'I too will do as you do, and I will command my sons and my brothers to bury me in the land of Canaan.' And Jacob said, 'Swear to me that you will do thus for me' — for I am not binding you by oath to command your sons likewise. And had the language 'to me' not been used, I would have said that he was binding him by oath concerning all his words, even concerning 'I will do as you have spoken.'" "Another matter: that Jacob wanted to conceal the oath from those standing by, for he said to him, 'Put your hand under my thigh' (Genesis 47:29), meaning, 'and swear to me.' Joseph said to him: 'I will do as you have spoken' (Genesis 47:30) — without an oath, for I am trustworthy in my word. And he said, 'Swear to me' (Genesis 47:31) — that is, to me alone, in secret, so that no person would know of this; for it would be a disgrace to Joseph to say that his father did not believe him and needed to bind him by oath. Therefore it says, 'and he swore to him' (Genesis 47:31) — to him alone." "Another matter: that at first he said to him, 'Put now your hand under my thigh' (Genesis 47:29), for he wanted to bind him by oath while grasping an object. And he said to him, 'I will do as you have spoken' — without an oath. And Jacob thought that Joseph was vexed that he sought to bind him by a severe oath, so he said, 'Swear to me' — without the grasping of an object, 'and he swore to him.' And therefore it is not stated here, 'and he placed his hand under his father's thigh,' in the manner stated concerning Eliezer the servant of Abraham (Genesis 24:9)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:07.212540239Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}